# Copy this file to .env, then fill in your own identity:
#
#   cp .devcontainer/.env.example .devcontainer/.env
#
# The .env file is gitignored, so it stays on your machine.
#
# Do not leave either value empty. Git reads these directly, and they outrank
# user.name and user.email. A blank name fails every commit with
# "fatal: empty ident name", and a blank email records an empty author email.
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="Your Name"
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="you@example.com"

# Provider API keys for the LiteLLM proxy (.devcontainer/litellm.yaml). Fill in
# and uncomment both keys if you can. deepseek-v4-pro is the default model, and
# when a DeepSeek request fails, LiteLLM retries it on claude-opus. That retry
# only works if the Anthropic key is set too. If you skip a key, leave it
# commented out, not blank.
#
# A change takes effect only when the container is recreated, not re-attached:
#
#   docker compose -f .devcontainer/compose.yaml up -d --force-recreate

# Anthropic: https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# DeepSeek: https://platform.deepseek.com/api_keys
# Some tools use DeepSeek through its Anthropic-style endpoint. They store
# this key as ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN and also set ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL. If you have
# those, copy only the key value to the line below. Do not add either name to
# this file or to compose.yaml. LiteLLM reads both names for its Anthropic
# models. ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL would send Claude requests to DeepSeek.
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN would send your DeepSeek key to Anthropic.
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=

# These matter on Linux hosts only, and only if your account is not uid 1000.
# A bind mount passes ownership through unchanged, so the container cannot
# write into /app. Uncomment these, set them to your own `id -u` and `id -g`,
# and rebuild:
#
#   docker compose -f .devcontainer/compose.yaml up -d --build
#
# Docker Desktop on Windows and macOS maps every file to the container user,
# so leave these alone there.
# USER_UID=1000
# USER_GID=1000
